[34.001257, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[25;2H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[26;73H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[27;73H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[28;63H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[30;73H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[31;8H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[32;2H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[33;2H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[34;27H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[35;2H\u001b[0m\u001b[m\u001b[K\u001b[25;1H \u001b[26;1H  \u001b[1mRoot cause:\u001b[22m In penalty.py, the alpha weights were applied to the wrong\u001b[27;1H  norms — alpha multiplied the L2 term and (1 - alpha) multiplied the L1\u001b[28;1H  term. By the standard elastic-net convention (and the tests'\u001b[29;1H  expectations), alpha is the L1 weight: alpha=1 → pure L1/lasso .7, alpha=0\u001b[30;1H  → pure L2/ridge .525. I verified this by computing both forms from the\u001b[31;1H  parts\u001b[32;1H \u001b[33;1H \u001b[34;1H\u001b[1m›\u001b[22m \u001b[2mAsk Codex to do anything\u001b[35;1H\u001b[22m \u001b[36;1H  nvidia/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP4-Local low · /tmp/aie-codex-tui/take1/worksp…\u001b[m\u001b[m\u001b[0m\u001b[25;1H\u001b[0 q\u001b[25;1H \u001b[m\u001b[m\u001b[0m\u001b[34;3H\u001b[?25h\u001b[?2026l"]
